TEXT:
CSRU Nursing Update
Neuro: Alert and oriented x3; MAE w/good strength; OOB and ambulate in hallway today; This evening around 1830, pt became anxious, legs twitching, reporting sternal incision pain-percocet given, ativan given-cont to be anxious;
CV: Pt apaced throughout day w/junctional rhythm under pacer, @ 1830 pt looks to be in Afib after a wire tracing done-rate 80's-[**Last Name (NamePattern4) 781**], NP notified; BP stable, becoming slightly hypertensive this evening; Skin warm, dry, pulses weak, palpable;
Resp: 3 Liters NC; Lungs clear, resps NL; Sats 98% or better; Using IS [**Telephone/Fax (1) 244**];
GI: Healthy appetite; Abd soft; Hypoactive BS;
GU: Foley in place, urine output adequate;
Endo: Glucose covered per sliding scale;
Plan: Monitor HR/BP/Anxiety; Cont activity;

npn 23:00-07:00

71M w/ hx CAD, afib, vasculitis, c/c fever and hypoxia

At beginning of this shift, team stated pt would be a call-out to regular floor;

However this night significant for occurance again of fever, rigors, developed wheezes only at this episode; received 500 NS IVF bolus to be able to give IV demerol 12.5 mg IV for rigors, sbp already mid 90's to low 100's; demerol effective in ceasing rigors, however returned again aprox 45 min later, dose repeated with again good effect;
   Pt on RA at 23:00 w/ O2 sats approx 96%; left on 4 l nc after alb neb treatments for wheezes w/ above described episode; desat'd at end of episode when sitting at bedside, returned to 92% when assisted to lay back down;
   Pt stated this is how his vasculitis episodes present; however OSH showed possible RLL infiltrate, therefore received Levoflox po as ordered at 04:00; pt also received po Prednisone 60 mg;
   This episode lasted approx 2 hours before pt appeared more comfortable again and was able to sleep;

At 02:00, pt also requested ativan to help him sleep, received 1 mg po as ordered (may have tid); pt routinely on ativan for pre-existing anxiety;

Attempted to draw all a.m. labs ordered, pt Very Difficult stick, pt's vascularization likely clamped down due to above episode; sent off specimens hoping QS; if needed, covering MD will draw a.m. labs via art stick;

Remains in a-fib, was on lopressor po 2xd, was dc'd due to pt's sbp on low side and v-response to 50's;

PLAN:
1) pt likely to remain in ICU care
2) follow Temps and sequelae
3) address pt concerns
4) check lab results

CSRU 7A-7P
Neuro: Alert and oriented x3; Slightly anxious about condition (mostly worried about lung status); No neuro deficits noted;
CV: Sinus rhythm-80-90's; BP variable throughout day-hypertensive (lopressor dose increased) BP cuff pressures 30 pts higher on L than right-Currently following L arm BP cuff pressures; Skin warm and dry-generalized edema; Palpable pulses; 2 units PRBC's infused today; Pacer wires attached to pacer, which is off;
Resp: Currently on 4 l NC; alternates using 35% shovel mask for humidity; Continuous Wet cough-little production thick yellow; Nebs Q6hr. initiated today; Lungs coarse, decreased @ bases bilat; Uses IS;
CT x 2 to sx w/o airleak;
GI: NAS/low choles. diet; appetite fair; abd soft, non-dist; active bowel sounds; no bm as of yet
GU: foley in place-u/o adequate/borderline throughout day-Lasix given ~5pm; Urine clear, yellow
Pain: Percocet for pain during day; Pain control changed to Dilaudid for questionable confusion for short period;
Activity: OOB for most of day;
Plan: Cont Pulm toilet; control hypertension;

npn 7p-7a (see also carevue flownotes for objective data)

dx: sepsis
56 yo w/ down's; trach, feeding tube; c3-4 spinal fusion; paraplegia;
hx MRSA sputum; fungal infections--skin;

pt off sedation yesterday morning; remains minimally responsive w/ opening eyes, no verbalizaion, no communication of any kind;

hemodynamically stable; NSR 80's-90's; does become hypertensive when 'bothered' for cares;

resp--remains on PS 16/5 0.40; requires approx q3 hr suctioning for whitish med thin secretions;

skin--[** **] under left [**Last Name (LF) **], [**First Name3 (LF) 52**]-area red; foley in use; mycostatin ointment ordered and received; will ask for mycostatin powder for peri-area;

g-i--tube feeds at goal; received senna early yest a.m., and lactulose yesterdy--stooled all night;

access--rt subclav [**First Name9 (NamePattern2) 1774**] [**Last Name (un) 1389**]; left radial aline; both with good wave forms;

a.m. labs much the same as yesterday morning;

PLAN:
1) urine output to be approx 100 cc/hr, lasix prn to assist
2) ativan as ordered q 4 hrs--especially for turning
3) stool sent for c-diff--pending
4) abx (meropenum) for GNR
5) tube feeds at goal--50 mls/hr
6) PICC Monday in IR (unable to do [**Last Name (un) 302**])--then d/c back to NE [**Hospital1 793**]
7) pt's brother is HCP
8) senna once every day to avoid lactulose

npn 7p-7a (see also carevue flownotes for objective data)

dx: sepsis        hx/o MRSA sputum
56 yo pt w/ Down's, reportedly vegetative state x 3 yrs; non communicative; dementia, sz d/o (improved w/ trileptic), freq UTI's, paraplegia, dysphagia--feeding tube, C3-4 spinal fusion; skin fungal infection x several months; trach/peg;

comes in for fevers/sepsis;
is receiving IV ABx of Meropenum for GNR;

rt subclav [**First Name9 (NamePattern2) 1774**] [**Last Name (un) 1389**] placed in ER [**7-3**]--as of yesterday, very reddened, +small amt exudate at insertion site, drssg changed yesterday and this a.m. at 04:30;

left radial a-line dc'd yesterday [**7-9**];

b/p borderline at times, therefore goal for diurese loosened; (no lasix given);

pt given senna and lactulose 2 days ago d/t no stool, then had continual diarrhea/stooling x 36 hrs, slowed down this night;

receiving tube feeds as ordered;

mycostatin ointment and powder alternately ordered and applied to skin fungal areas;

this a.m.'s labs w/out significant change, except for decreased in WBC this a.m. in 3K's;

PLAN:
1) pt to receive PICC line in IR today/Monday (unable to do at [**Month/Year (2) 302**])
2) return to NE [**Hospital1 793**] after placement of PICC
3) stool sent for c-diff pending
4) skin care
5) brother HCP, sister also involved
6) IV Abx (note allergies)
